{"text":"Recent work supports separating global transition structure from local sequence modeling. [TGCL4SR (AAAI 2024)](https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/28789) combines temporal item-transition graphs, time-aware sequence encoding and graph contrastive learning. [GLSRec (2026)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.datak.2026.102606) combines global transition-graph embeddings with local MLP sequence modeling and interest-aware hard negatives. These provide interaction-based architectural ideas compatible with learning from scratch; their published results do not establish performance under this benchmark.

Text-based approaches often violate the available-resource constraints: [ITS-Rec (2025)](https://www.mdpi.com/2079-9292/14/9/1748) uses BERT representations and reviews; [GALLM (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.12184) introduces text–text, item–text and collaborative item–item attention relations within an LLM. Their transferable idea is to preserve distinct content and collaborative relations, rather than equating textual similarity with next-item relevance. [Revisiting Graph Projections (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09209) offers a particularly relevant lightweight alternative: infer complementary-product scores using directed, weighted projections of historical user–item interactions.

Suggested synthesis—not a directly validated published method: retain SASRec, add a jointly trained title/description token encoder and a separate directed transition scorer, then learn a gate combining sequence, content and transition scores. Train text representations only on supplied metadata and training interactions. Use asymmetric source/target embeddings to represent directional relations; compare against symmetric text similarity through ablations. Co-occurrence and purchase order provide uncertain evidence of complementarity, not compatibility or prerequisite labels. Select variants exclusively by the common macro validation HR@10."}
